# SPACBR — AUR packages
#
# Per §41-43 of CLAUDE.md: only add an entry when a specific, justified
# need can't be met officially, and note that justification next to
# it. The installer needs exactly one AUR helper (paru) to build
# these — don't add a second. paru itself is bootstrapped
# automatically (install_aur_helper in install/functions/packages.sh)
# if it isn't already present.
#
# arc-gtk-theme is NOT listed here even though it's an AUR package and
# SPACBR depends on it (every gtk-2.0/3.0/4.0 config and xinitrc's
# GTK_THEME assume Arc-Dark). It's built instead from
# packages/aur-overrides/arc-gtk-theme/, a SPACBR-maintained PKGBUILD
# fork -- verified for real that the published AUR PKGBUILD fails to
# build outright (hardcodes GNOME Shell 43 theming against a source
# tarball that doesn't have that asset layout). See that PKGBUILD's
# header for the full story, and the "GTK theme installed" doctor
# check in install/functions/checks.sh, which verifies
# /usr/share/themes/Arc-Dark actually exists on disk regardless of
# which path installed it.
#
# Add an entry here only for a plain, unmodified AUR package that
# doesn't need packages/aur-overrides treatment.

localsend
# localsend (source build), not localsend-bin: -bin was out-of-date as
# of 2026-08-21 when checked; the source package wasn't. Previously
# excluded from this file entirely (see below) as outside SPACBR's
# capability list -- explicitly requested since, so that reasoning no
# longer applies here specifically (the general "don't add apps
# speculatively" principle still does everywhere else).

netbird-bin
# -bin, not source: verified for real (2026-08-25) that netbird-bin
# was exactly version-matched with the source package (0.77.1-1) --
# unlike localsend-bin above, which was genuinely behind. Building
# netbird from source is a multi-minute Go compile (a large dependency
# tree resolved fresh from proxy.golang.org every time); a version-
# matched -bin skips all of that for zero real cost right now. Same
# caveat as always with any -bin package though: nothing guarantees it
# stays in sync the way localsend-bin didn't -- re-check
# `paru -Si netbird-bin` against the source package's version if this
# is ever revisited, don't just assume this holds forever.
#
# Mesh VPN, replacing tailscale (which was an official package,
# packages/base). Not available officially -- confirmed not in
# core/extra/multilib as of 2026-08-25, AUR-only. WireGuard-based like
# Tailscale, same "one mesh VPN" slot in the capability list (§44) --
# not a second owner alongside Tailscale, a straight swap. Ships its
# own `netbird@main.service` systemd template unit (enabled by
# setup_netbird() in services.sh, same "enable the daemon, don't run
# the interactive login" split setup_tailscale used). Per NetBird's own
# docs (docs.netbird.io/about-netbird/ports-and-firewalls), the client
# needs no inbound firewall port at all in a standard NAT'd deployment
# -- purely outbound STUN/relay negotiation -- and manages its own
# separate nftables table for the `wt0` interface rather than needing
# an entry in system/nftables/nftables.conf's table, unlike Tailscale's
# `iifname "tailscale0"` rule. See that file's comment for the one
# thing worth watching: this repo's own ruleset does a `flush ruleset`
# on every load, and whether that could ever race against netbird's
# own dynamically-created table is unverified, not confirmed safe.

mpdris2-rs
# mpd doesn't expose MPRIS on its own (verified for real: no
# org.mpris.* name on the session bus at all without this), which
# silently broke dwm's playerctl-based media keys for mpd and left
# fastfetch's Player/Media modules with nothing to read. Rust, not the
# older Python mpdris2 (also available) -- smaller dependency
# footprint for what's just a small D-Bus bridge daemon. Enabled by
# setup_mpd() in services.sh, conditional on mpd being installed at
# all like everything else that function does.

brave-origin-nightly-bin
helium-browser-bin
zen-browser-bin
# Explicitly requested, so treated the same way localsend's own entry
# above already explains: previously excluded (see the "considered and
# left out" list below) as three more browsers competing with
# Firefox's "Browser" ownership (§7) -- that reasoning is a default,
# not a lock, and doesn't apply once someone's actually asked for
# these specifically. Firefox stays the one thing dwm/dmenu/xdg-mime
# etc. actually wire up as the default browser (same "one thing owns
# the default, alternatives are just available" shape as Zed alongside
# Neovim, or alacritty alongside st) -- these three are launched like
# any other app through the dmenu launcher (MODKEY+p), not given
# dedicated keybindings or made the xdg default. All three confirmed
# present in the AUR as of 2026-08-25 (brave-origin-nightly-bin
# 1.96.10-1, helium-browser-bin 0.15.7.1-1, zen-browser-bin 1.21.15b-1)
# -- re-check they still exist if this is ever revisited; -bin AUR
# packages for fast-moving browsers churn names/maintainers more than
# most.

